# Q1 - Experimental Time Complexity Analysis

## Aim

To experimentally determine the worst-case time complexity of different
operations on different data structure representations.

The following representations are studied:

1. Unsorted Array
2. Sorted Array
3. Singly Linked List - Unsorted
4. Singly Linked List - Sorted
5. Doubly Linked List - Unsorted
6. Doubly Linked List - Sorted

The operations studied are:

- Search
- Insert
- Delete
- Maximum
- Minimum
- Predecessor
- Successor

---

## Experimental Method

The program tests multiple input sizes from n = 64 to n = 65536.

Instead of using clock time, the program counts the dominant operations
performed by each algorithm. This avoids noise caused by the system
timer and gives a more consistent measurement.

The measured growth is compared with:

- O(1) - Constant
- O(log n) - Logarithmic
- O(n) - Linear

---

## Theoretical Worst-Case Complexity

| Representation | Search | Insert | Delete | Maximum | Minimum | Predecessor | Successor |
|----------------|--------|--------|--------|---------|---------|-------------|-----------|
| Unsorted Array | O(n) | O(1) | O(1) | O(n) | O(n) | O(n) | O(n) |
| Sorted Array | O(log n) | O(n) | O(n) | O(1) | O(1) | O(1) | O(1) |
| Singly Linked Unsorted | O(n) | O(1) | O(n) | O(n) | O(n) | O(n) | O(n) |
| Singly Linked Sorted | O(n) | O(n) | O(n) | O(1) | O(1) | O(n) | O(1) |
| Doubly Linked Unsorted | O(n) | O(1) | O(1) | O(n) | O(n) | O(n) | O(n) |
| Doubly Linked Sorted | O(n) | O(n) | O(1) | O(1) | O(1) | O(1) | O(1) |

---

## Program

The C program:

- Creates the required data structures.
- Generates different input sizes.
- Performs the required operations.
- Counts the dominant operations.
- Compares the experimental growth with the theoretical complexity.
- Generates experimental data for plotting.
- Reports the experimentally inferred complexity.

---

## Experimental Data

The program generates the file:

q1_experimental_data.dat

This file contains the measured dominant operation counts for the
different input sizes and operations.

---

## Graph

The graph contains six panels, one for each data structure
representation.

Each panel shows the growth of the seven operations:

- Search
- Insert
- Delete
- Maximum
- Minimum
- Predecessor
- Successor

The graph is used to compare the experimental growth with the expected
complexity classes O(1), O(log n), and O(n).

---

## Observation

The experimental results follow the expected theoretical growth
patterns.

Operations classified as O(1) remain approximately constant as n
increases.

Operations classified as O(log n) grow slowly with n.

Operations classified as O(n) increase approximately linearly with n.

---

## Conclusion

The experiment demonstrates that the worst-case time complexity of an
operation depends on both the operation and the data structure
representation.

The experimental results are consistent with the theoretical
complexity table.

The experiment also shows that choosing an appropriate data structure
can significantly affect the efficiency of an operation.

---
